-- title:
--   Unit discriminant gives 𝒪[c] as the integral closure
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ and $L$ be fields with $L$ an $F$-algebra, let $\mathcal{O}$ be a valuation subring of $F$, and suppose $L$ is also an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra compatibly, i.e. $\mathcal{O} \to F \to L$ is a scalar tower. Let $c \in L$ be integral over $\mathcal{O}$, assume that the intermediate field of $L$ generated over $F$ by $c$ is all of $L$ (so $L = F(c)$), and assume that the norm $N_{L/F}\bigl(f'(c)\bigr)$, where $f = \mathrm{minpoly}_F(c)$ and $f'$ is its derivative, lies in the image of the unit group $\mathcal{O}^\times$ under $\mathcal{O} \to F$, i.e. there is $u \in \mathcal{O}^\times$ with $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,u = N_{L/F}(f'(c))$. Then every $z \in L$ which is integral over $\mathcal{O}$ belongs to the $\mathcal{O}$-subalgebra $\mathcal{O}[c]$ of $L$ generated by $c$. Thus, under a unit-discriminant hypothesis, the integral closure of $\mathcal{O}$ in $L$ is contained in (hence equals) $\mathcal{O}[c]$.
--
--   This is Dedekind's discriminant criterion for monogenicity, in the local form over a valuation ring: a unit value of $N_{L/F}(f'(c))$ forces $\mathcal{O}[c]$ to be integrally closed in $L = F(c)$. It is used in the analysis of places of curves, where it feeds [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.ramificationIndexAlong_eq_one_of_pow_eq_of_mem_of_inv_mem`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.ramificationIndexAlong_eq_one_of_pow_eq_of_mem_of_inv_mem), i.e. the unramifiedness of Kummer-type extensions generated by an $n$-th root of a unit.
